# A1: Yes, focus on DUPs.

Understand DUPs fundamentally and implement, from first principles, whatever
machinery is needed to ensure DUPs don't interrupt fast mode. Stick to wnf() as
the reference interpreter and semantics — do NOT invent anything that detours
from it. The commit mentioned in GOAL attempted this and may have valuable
insights.

You're free to focus 100% on this. I highly recommend spending your time
accumulating domain knowledge and only making edits when you're 100% certain
you have the correct fundamental structure. Measure twice, cut once.

Also: rewrite things from scratch as soon as you believe doing so would result
in a better version. Do NOT patch a worse foundation if you have a better one
in mind. Rewrites are a VERY good use of your time.

# A2: Static APP-LAM should absolutely be inlinable.

When the function is a static lambda, treating it as a let is observationally
equivalent. If that's not the case, something weird is going on and it's most
likely a misimplementation. So yes, this is aligned — but make sure the
implementation is actually correct and not doing something unexpected.
